WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 195: Bill Clinton: The Economic Boom, the Government Shutdowns, and Socks the Cat (1993–2001)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.280
[Rebecca] Today we step into the prosperous whirlwind world of Bill Clinton's second term.

4
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:20.240
[Rebecca] In an era of peace, balanced budgets, and the dawn of the internet, Clinton navigated government shutdowns and impeachment with sheer political vitality.

5
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:30.560
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the 1990s boom, the battle with Newt Gingrich, and the famous White House cat named Sox.

6
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:45.280
[George] When Bill Clinton assumed the presidency in 1993, he inherited a staggering $290 billion annual federal budget deficit left over from the 1980s that threatened to strangle long-term economic investment.

7
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:52.240
[George] Urged by economic advisors Robert Rubin and Alan Greenspan, Clinton took a monumental political gamble.

8
00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:05.440
[George] He scrapped his planned middle class tax cut and proposed the Deficit Reduction Act of 1993, combining deep spending cuts with higher tax rates on the wealthiest 1% of corporations and individuals.

9
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:12.080
[George] Congressional Republicans fiercely opposed the bill, predicting it would plunge the nation into an immediate recession.

10
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:25.600
[George] In August 1993, the bill passed without a single Republican vote, squeaking through the House by two votes and passing the Senate on a dramatic 50-50 tiebreaker vote cast by Vice President Al Gore.

11
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.640
[George] The gamble paid off with historic brilliance.

12
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:37.200
[George] Long-term interest rates plummeted, business confidence soared, and the foundation was laid for the greatest economic boom in modern history.

13
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:49.040
[George] That bold fiscal legislation proved that disciplined deficit reduction paired with progressive taxation could stimulate investment, tame inflation, and unleash private sector job creation.

14
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:58.240
[George] Clinton's economic policies created an environment where American technological ingenuity and private enterprise could flourish on an unprecedented scale.

15
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:07.800
[George] The fiscal discipline of the Clinton years proved that a nation could balance its books, invest in education, and spur technological growth simultaneously.

16
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:16.360
[George] That momentous legislative vote set the stage for eight years of record capital investment and rising real wages for American workers.

17
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:25.000
[George] In the midterm elections of November 1994, the American electorate delivered a stinging repudiation to the Clinton administration.

18
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:37.080
[George] Led by fiery Georgia Congressman Newt Gingrich and his ten-point contract with America, Republicans captured control of both the House of Representatives and the Senate for the first time in 40 years.

19
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:46.280
[George] Jingrich became Speaker of the House, promising to roll back the New Deal and slash federal spending on Medicare, Medicaid, and education.

20
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:58.760
[George] When Clinton refused to sign their budget cuts in late 1995, Jingrich shut down the federal government twice, shuttering national parks, museums and federal offices for 28 days.

21
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:10.280
[George] Jingrich publicly complained to reporters that Clinton had snubbed him on Air Force One on the return flight from Israeli Prime Minister Yitzhak Rabin's funeral by making him exit through the rear door.

22
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:21.560
[George] Clinton seized the moral high ground, appearing calm, presidential and reasonable, while public opinion turned overwhelmingly against the Republican leadership for closing the government.

23
00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:32.200
[George] The public backlash against the shutdowns showed that the American electorate desired constructive bipartisan governance and pragmatic solutions rather than ideological brinkmanship.

24
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:41.240
[George] The resolution of the budget showdown established the executive branch as the essential guardian of essential public services and environmental protection.

25
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:50.000
[George] The government shutdowns demonstrated that the presidency remains the essential constitutional counterbalance against reckless legislative overreach.

26
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:58.800
[George] The American people recognized that shutting down the government was an irresponsible tactic that harmed innocent federal employees and citizens alike.

27
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:13.840
[George] Advised by political strategist Dick Morris, Clinton embraced a political strategy known as triangulation, positioning the presidency above both traditional liberal Democrats and conservative Republicans by adopting popular centrist solutions.

28
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:23.040
[George] In his January 1996 State of the Union Address, Clinton stunned Washington by declaring, the era of big government is over.

29
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:32.320
[George] Partnering with the Republican Congress, Clinton signed the landmark Personal Responsibility and Work Opportunity Reconciliation Act of 1996.

30
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.360
[George] Reforming welfare by requiring work in exchange for time-limited assistance.

31
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:46.240
[George] In November 1996, Clinton easily won re-election over Republican Senator Bob Dole, becoming the first Democrat since Franklin D.

32
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.400
[George] Roosevelt to win two terms.

33
00:04:48.400 --> 00:05:05.440
[George] By 1998, fueled by the explosion of the commercial Internet, personal computing, and low unemployment, the federal budget was completely balanced for the first time since 1969, generating record-setting surpluses of over 200 billion dollars.

34
00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:15.920
[George] The era of balanced federal budgets demonstrated the extraordinary productive power of an innovative, tech driven economy operating in harmony with prudent fiscal policy.

35
00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:25.440
[George] The 1990s boom lifted millions of families out of poverty, creating widespread upward mobility across all segments of the American workforce.

36
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:33.880
[George] That extraordinary decade of prosperity proved the boundless potential of the American workforce when paired with innovation and sound governance.

37
00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:41.640
[George] The resulting budget surpluses allowed the federal government to begin paying down the national debt while funding education and science.

38
00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:55.560
[George] Amidst the high-stakes legislative warfare, the Clinton White House was humanized by one of the most famous feline celebrities in history, Sox, an affectionate tuxedo stray adopted by Chelsea Clinton in Arkansas.

39
00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:07.880
[George] Sox became a worldwide media sensation, roaming the West Wing, sleeping on the leather armchairs of the Oval Office, and perching atop the White House press room podium for hilarious photo shoots.

40
00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:17.720
[George] Sox received thousands of fan letters from children across the globe, requiring his own dedicated volunteer staff to respond with stamped pawprint signatures.

41
00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:29.240
[George] In 1997, the family introduced a chocolate Labrador retriever puppy named Buddy, igniting an epic, comedic domestic cold war inside the executive mansion.

42
00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:40.200
[George] Sox fiercely hated Buddy, arching his back and hissing whenever the puppy bounded into the room, forcing secret service agents to keep the two pets in separate wings of the mansion.

43
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:49.720
[George] The playful rivalry between Sox and Buddy brought warmth and humor to the executive mansion, reminding millions that even the White House is a home with pets.

44
00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:59.400
[George] Sox and Buddy became beloved cultural ambassadors, generating immense public affection and raising millions of dollars for animal charities nationwide.

45
00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:08.120
[George] The affection showered on the presidential pets was a joyful reminder of the warmth and humanity that reside inside the walls of the executive mansion.

46
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:16.240
[George] The public delight in the White House pets demonstrated that common human warmth unites Americans far more deeply than political differences.

47
00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:25.440
[George] In foreign policy, Clinton deployed American prestige and military power to halt brutal ethnic cleansing and resolve generational conflicts.

48
00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.400
[George] In 1995, following the horrific S.

49
00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.640
[George] Rebrenica massacre in Bosnia.

50
00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:47.040
[George] Clinton authorized NATO airstrikes and locked the warring Balkan leaders, Serbian President Slobodin Myloshvik, Croatian President Franjo Tudman, and Bosnian President Alija Ispigovic, inside Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Dayton, Ohio.

51
00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:56.400
[George] For three grueling weeks, American diplomats led by Richard Holbrook hammered out the historic Dayton Peace Accords, ending the bloody Bosnian War.

52
00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:04.240
[George] In 1998, Clinton played a crucial personal role in negotiating the historic Good Friday Agreement in Northern Ireland.

53
00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:16.720
[George] Speaking directly by telephone with Protestant and Catholic leaders to bring 30 years of sectarian violence known as the Troubles to an end, demonstrating a remarkable gift for personal reconciliation.

54
00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:27.120
[George] The Dayton Accords and Good Friday Agreement demonstrated that relentless American diplomatic engagement and moral leadership can halt centuries of sectarian bloodshed.

55
00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:35.840
[George] The diplomatic breakthroughs in the Balkans and Northern Ireland proved that American power is at its most potent when wielded in the service of human peace.

56
00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:42.960
[George] Those historic peace agreements showed that American diplomacy can build bridges between enemies who have fought for generations.

57
00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:51.360
[George] The successful interventions in the Balkans proved that decisive American leadership can uphold human rights and stop genocide in the heart of Europe.

58
00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:57.920
[George] In 1998, Clinton's presidency was nearly destroyed by a sensational personal scandal.

59
00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:12.920
[George] Independent Council Kenneth Starr's multi-year investigation expanded to examine allegations that Clinton had engaged in an extramarital affair with 22-year-old White House intern Monica Lewinsky and lied about it under oath in a civil deposition.

60
00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:26.600
[George] In December 1998, the House of Representatives voted along strict party lines to impeach Clinton on charges of perjury and obstruction of justice, making him only the second president in American history to be impeached.

61
00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:34.440
[George] But in the Senate trial in February 1999, the prosecution fell far short of the required two-thirds majority.

62
00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:42.600
[George] Not a single Democratic senator voted for conviction, and several moderate Republicans crossed party lines to acquit him on both articles.

63
00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:56.440
[George] Throughout the ordeal, Clinton maintained an astonishing, compartmentalized focus on governance, presiding over 22 million new jobs, 4% unemployment, and the highest consumer confidence in 30 years.

64
00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:06.280
[George] Throughout the intense pressure of the impeachment trial, Clinton's disciplined focus on the nation's business kept the economy soaring and unemployment at historic lows.

65
00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:14.920
[George] His acquittal by the Senate reaffirmed the high constitutional bar required to overturn the democratic verdict of a national presidential election.

66
00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:23.880
[George] His survival of impeachment proved that the constitutional system of checks and balances was designed to withstand the fiercest storms of partisan passion.

67
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:32.760
[George] Clinton's resilience during the trial proved to the world that the executive branch could continue functioning effectively despite immense political turbulence.

68
00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:44.880
[George] When Bill Clinton departed the White House on January 20, 2001, at the age of 54, he left the United States at the pinnacle of global power, peace, and prosperity.

69
00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:49.040
[George] The national debt was on track to be completely paid off within a decade.

70
00:10:49.280 --> 00:11:00.800
[George] The Internet had transformed global commerce, and his public approval rating stood at 66%, the highest departure rating of any two-term president in the post-World War II era.

71
00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:09.760
[George] In his farewell address, Clinton urged the country to preserve the hard-won fiscal discipline and embrace the interconnected global future with confidence.

72
00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:14.400
[George] You have given me the gift of a lifetime, and for that you will always have my heart.

73
00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.040
[George] He moved to Chapaca, New York.

74
00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:30.480
[George] Establishing the Clinton Foundation to fight global HIV AIDS, poverty and climate change, cementing his status as one of the most resilient, brilliant, and consequential political figures of modern times.

75
00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:40.160
[George] Bill Clinton departed the White House, leaving an economy transformed by technology, historic surpluses, and an enduring standard of political resilience.

76
00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:49.680
[George] Bill Clinton left behind a vibrant nation standing proudly at the dawn of the 21st century, confident in its economic strength and global leadership.

77
00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:58.080
[George] Bill Clinton left the American people with an unshakable optimism that tomorrow could always be better than today if we have the courage to work for it.

78
00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:07.760
[George] His final years in office stood as a high watermark of modern peace and prosperity, setting a benchmark for 21st century economic success.

79
00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:16.320
[Rebecca] Bill Clinton presided over a golden era of economic expansion and balanced budgets, leaving the nation prosperous and peaceful.

80
00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:29.640
[Rebecca] To succeed him in 21, the American people witnessed the closest, most contentious election in modern history, bringing a Texas governor to the White House, whose presidency would be defined by the smoke of Ground zero.

81
00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:30.600
[Rebecca] George W.

82
00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:31.640
[Rebecca] Bush.

83
00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:35.160
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the dramatic presidency of George W.

84
00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:43.960
[Rebecca] Bush, the Florida recount, the tragedy of September 11, the bullhorn at Ground Zero, and the historic first pitch at Yankee Stadium.

85
00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:48.360
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

